# R2D2's opencode server -- environment for scripts/r2d2-opencode.service.
#
# Copy to .env.oc, fill in the password, and make it private:
#
#   cp .env.oc.example .env.oc
#   chmod 0600 .env.oc
#   $EDITOR .env.oc
#
# `bash scripts/install.sh` does that copy for you, and never overwrites an
# .env.oc that already exists.
#
# .env.oc holds a live credential and MUST stay at mode 0600 and git-ignored.
# It is git-ignored (see .gitignore); this example file is the committed one and
# must never contain a real value.
#
# systemd does NOT expand ${HOME} in this file: it reads every value literally.
# scripts/opencode_serve.sh expands the three home spellings itself (~, $HOME,
# ${HOME}), so the values below work as written -- and an absolute path works
# just as well, which is what you need if your workspace is outside $HOME.
#
# R2D2 must never be started without a password: scripts/opencode_serve.sh
# refuses to start and exits 1 when R2D2_OC_PASSWORD is empty, because
# opencode's server auth is HTTP basic via OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD and without
# it the server answers every route on a plain HTTP port to every process on
# this box.
#
# The SAME password must also reach R2D2 itself, because R2D2 authenticates to
# that server as a client: R2D2_OC_PASSWORD and R2D2_OC_USERNAME are expanded
# into config/backends.json from R2D2's own environment (its .env). Setting
# them here only arms the server; R2D2 needs them too.
#
# Enabling the unit is a manual step and is deliberately not done by any script:
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload
#   systemctl --user enable --now r2d2-opencode

# Port the server listens on. Passed to the binary explicitly, never defaulted:
# the docs say 4096 and `serve --help` on this build says 0.
R2D2_OC_PORT=4599

# HTTP basic username. opencode's own default is `opencode`; set it explicitly
# so the client and the server cannot drift apart.
R2D2_OC_USERNAME=opencode

# The shared HTTP basic password. Generate one, do not invent one:
#   openssl rand -base64 24
# Leave blank here -- the launcher refuses to start on a blank password, and a
# committed value would be a credential in git history.
R2D2_OC_PASSWORD=

# Directory the server runs in, and therefore the directory every session it
# creates points at (U5). Must exist; the launcher will not create it, and
# scripts/install.sh creates exactly this one.
R2D2_OC_WORKSPACE=${HOME}/r2d2-workspace

# Optional. Directory holding R2D2's opencode.json, written by
# scripts/install.sh. Note it MERGES with the global config rather than
# isolating it, so its permission rules are R2D2's only defence and it must be
# installed.
# R2D2_OC_CONFIG_DIR=${HOME}/.r2d2/opencode

# Optional. Pin the opencode build. Without it the launcher takes
# ~/.opencode/bin/opencode (where opencode's own installer puts it), and falls
# back to the single `opencode` found on PATH -- refusing when PATH holds more
# than one, because "whichever sorts first" is how a stale build takes the
# port. Set this when you installed the CLI by other means, or when you want a
# specific version. Do not point it at a system package or a user npm prefix
# you have not checked with `opencode --version`.
# R2D2_OC_BIN=${HOME}/.opencode/bin/opencode
